  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""{
    "Introduction and Installing Python": "Computers execute step-by-step instructions. Programming languages recognize plain text files utilizing American Standard Code for Information Interchange (ASCII) character encoding for numbers, letters, and symbols. Python 3.12 or later can be downloaded and installed across Windows, Mac, and other platforms. Installing the Python interpreter automatically installs IDLE, an integrated development environment providing a Python shell for interactive mode testing, alongside text editor script mode. In IDLE syntax highlighting, keywords appear in orange, comments in red, string literals in green, and built-in functions in purple. Python is a high-level language utilizing an interpreter (which translates and runs high-level code) rather than a compiler, allowing developers to build programs without needing to understand underlying computer hardware. Program errors include syntax errors (caught by IDLE) and runtime errors (which occur while the program is running, causing Python to halt execution and display informative error traceback text). Helpful official web resources include the Python Software Foundation and Python Documentation.",
    "Introduction to Programming and Variables": "Programming is writing computer programs using a straightforward, object-oriented language like Python. Prewritten programs are called built-in functions, such as print(argument), which outputs string literals enclosed in single ('') or double (\"\") quotes (delimiters). Multiple print arguments are separated by spaces, and formatting features include sep, \\n for new lines, escape characters \\' and \\\", and end to prevent advancing lines. Long strings can span multiple lines using a trailing backslash. Comments use the # symbol for single lines or triple quotes for multi-line comments. Variables are symbolic names storing changeable values assigned using the = assignment operator. Variable naming rules dictate they are case-sensitive, cannot contain spaces, must begin with a letter or underscore (followed by letters, digits, or underscores), should be descriptive, limited to 3 or 4 words as a rule of thumb, and must not use Python keywords (e.g., and, def, else, for, if, in, not, or, return, while). Python supports numerical types like integers and floats, created via int() and float() constructors, and permits mixed arithmetic operations.",
    "Decision Structures": "Decision structures (conditional statements or if-then-else statements) alter program execution paths based on logical conditions or questions. A colon is required after both if and else statements, and strict indentation is mandatory for code blocks inside branches. Comparison operators evaluate numerical relationships such as less than, less than or equal to, greater than, greater than or equal to, equal to, and not equal to. Conditional statements can also incorporate logical relational operators and can be nested to handle complex multi-branched decision scenarios.",
    "Repetitions and Loops": "Repetition structures (loops) execute blocks of code repeatedly, avoiding the need to duplicate code sequences. A while loop repeats its indented body as long as its Boolean condition remains true; once false, control exits immediately to the following statement. If a condition remains perpetually true, the program enters an infinite loop. A for loop iterates a specified number of times, commonly utilizing a variable name inside range(number of times to repeat). Nested loops place one loop inside another (commonly used for two-dimensional structures), requiring the inner loop to complete all iterations fully for every single iteration of the outer loop.",
    "Functions and Recursion": "Functions are named, reusable code blocks that run only when explicitly called using their name followed by parentheses (). Function definitions begin with the def keyword header, require indented body blocks, and can receive arguments. Variables created inside a function are local variables, inaccessible outside that specific function scope. Functions are categorized as void functions (which execute and terminate without returning a value) or value-returning functions (which conclude with a return statement to pass a value back to the caller, and can return multiple values). Recursion occurs when a function calls itself to break down complex problems into simpler, structurally identical sub-problems, requiring a base case to stop the chain of recursive calls and prevent infinite recursion.",
    "File Access": "Files store persistent data, with text files encoding characters using ASCII or Unicode formatting. Files can be accessed sequentially (reading from start to end without skipping) or randomly. To use a file in Python, it must first be opened using open(), read from or written to, and permanently closed at the end of the program to free system memory. Reading operations include open(filename, 'r'), readline() for a single line, read() for the entire file, or read(n) for n bytes. Writing operations utilize open(filename, 'w') to create a new file (overwriting existing ones) or open(filename, 'a') to append data to an existing file, with data written as strings or via f-formatting, and files finalized using the write() and close() methods. The str() function converts numerical values to strings."
}"""
